[h1]Sublime Porte: Ottoman Reforged[/h1]

Play the Ottoman Empire as a nineteenth-century power that still has to earn every year of survival. This is a vanilla-first flavor and rework: a historically grounded 1836, a reform campaign you can see and steer, and three late-game identities that do not gift you an easy great power.

Strength still has to be earned through reform, investment, politics, diplomacy, and time.

[h2]The campaign you will play[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Campaign Tree.[/b] Open it from the sidebar. Tanzimat leads to the Constitutional Question, then to Three Policies — Ottomanism, Islamism, and Turkism — with live requirements so you always know what the next step actually is.
[*][b]Tanzimat and the Sick Man.[/b] Objectives track finances, literacy, secession, army, and administration. Optional one-shot decrees (cizye, çiftlik, maarif, the slave market) spend treasury and political capital instead of waiting for the journal to tick. Success is a limited rejuvenation plus one specialization. Failure stays severe and no longer kills the ruler.
[*][b]Learning from Europe.[/b] Send students, officers, technicians, and educators to France, Britain, Prussia/Germany, or Austria. Choose a primary partner, live with complications, and later draw on expertise such as von der Goltz. You cannot run every mission at once.
[*][b]After Tanzimat.[/b] Young Ottomans, the Kanun-i Esasi, or a palace path. The three policies unlock only after that constitutional question closes. No scripted 1908 railroad.
[/list]

[h2]Three late-game identities[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Ottomanism.[/b] Equality, press, franchise, and a constitutional empire. No conquest tree — the prize is a harder, more coherent country.
[*][b]Islamism.[/b] Caliphate, Arabia, the Nile and Maghreb, then a protector of aligned Muslim states. The Caliph can call for support; it is not a forced holy war. Ends as a Great Caliphate on the Ottoman tag.
[*][b]Turkism and the Turanian Ideal.[/b] A long, staged claim path across the Turkic world. Remain the Ottoman Empire, or proclaim the Great Ottoman Empire (name, color, and flag). The country is never renamed Turan. Turkic cultures already share a kinship tradition from day one.
[/list]

[h2]1836, diplomacy, and the provinces[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Opening setup.[/b] Population, cultures, and religions redistributed across Ottoman-owned states (vanilla total kept). Selected resources and 1836 farms, workshops, ports, and military industry revised. Four armies reorganized on a documented order of battle. Nineteenth-century names; corrected commanders. Unused deposits are not free factories; Anatolian tea plantations are gone.
[*][b]The Egyptian Question.[/b] A limited Levant settlement, a costly maximalist victory, or a stalemate. No forced historical victor. Great Powers do not casually join Mehmed Ali.
[*][b]Hünkâr İskelesi and Baltalimanı.[/b] An opening Russian guarantee that later lapses, and an 1838 British trade privilege you can sign or refuse.
[*][b]The Eastern Question in peacetime.[/b] Russia presses the Orthodox question, a mid-century protectorship demand, and later eastern reform. Pressure and prestige — not a scripted Crimean War.
[*][b]Rumelia and the Great Eastern Crisis.[/b] Balkan guidance for reform, order, or compromise with existing subjects, plus crisis-scope corrections. The crisis itself remains National Awakening content when that DLC is enabled.
[*][b]Eastern provinces and the periphery.[/b] Kurdish emirates and Bedirhan; an Armenian Question after Berlin; Tripoli, Tunis, and an Aden alarm.
[*][b]Muhacir settlement.[/b] Timed waves from Crimea, the Caucasus, and Rumelia. Settlement choices and treasury costs — not free manpower.
[/list]

[h2]Court, companies, and flavor[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Topkapı and Dolmabahçe.[/b] Start in the old palace, build Dolmabahçe, move the court, optionally turn Topkapı into a museum.
[*][b]Everyday Porte.[/b] Schools, telegraph, press, advisers, railways, petitions, ports, and translation. Quiet years still feel Ottoman.
[*][b]Characters.[/b] Âlî, Fuad, Ahmed Cevdet, and Nâmık Kemal; later Fevzi Çakmak, Mustafa Kemal, and Kâzım Karabekir. Enver remains vanilla Voice of the People content.
[*][b]Şirket-i Hayriye.[/b] A Bosphorus port company once technology and Constantinople port capacity are in place.
[*][b]Imperial Ottoman Bank.[/b] A later one-shot concession: cheaper credit at a prestige cost, or a refusal that keeps the treasury in Ottoman hands.
[*][b]Music.[/b] Five original mood tracks composed for this mod, in the music player while you play as the empire.
[/list]

[h2]Requirements[/h2]
[b]Required mods[/b]
[list]
[*]Community Mod Framework — https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3385002128
[*]Community State Framework — https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3654153829
[/list]

[b]Load order:[/b] Community Mod Framework → Community State Framework → Sublime Porte: Ottoman Reforged

[b]National Awakening[/b] is optional. The mod is built to load with that DLC off.
[list]
[*][b]DLC on:[/b] Great Eastern Crisis journal content remains vanilla National Awakening content. This mod’s crisis-scope corrections apply when that journal is active.
[*][b]DLC off:[/b] the campaign still runs. Great Eastern Crisis journal content does not appear. Tanzimat, the Campaign Tree, Learning from Europe, 1836 setup, and other non-DLC features remain.
[/list]

Voice of the People and Sphere of Influence are also optional.

[h2]Compatibility[/h2]
This mod does [b]not[/b] use [c]replace_path[/c].

It does change selected Ottoman history, state-region, Tanzimat, Sick Man, Great Eastern Crisis, military, character, and naming files where vanilla has no additive hook. Compatibility with other large Ottoman overhaul or flavor mods that edit the same areas is [b]not guaranteed[/b]. Place this mod after Community Mod Framework and Community State Framework, and check load order if another Ottoman mod is in the playset.

[h2]Recommended[/h2]
Start a [b]new campaign[/b]. Installing the mod into an existing save will not rebuild the 1836 demographic, economic, naming, commander, or military setup.

[h2]Version[/h2]
[list]
[*]Mod version: see this Workshop item’s latest update (repository metadata uses semantic versioning)
[*]Victoria 3: [b]1.13.*[/b]
[*]Tested on: [b]1.13.11[/b]
[/list]

[h2]Languages[/h2]
English is the source language. Turkish is fully supported.

[h2]Credits and dependencies[/h2]
Victoria 3 is published by Paradox Interactive.

This release depends on [b]Community Mod Framework[/b] and [b]Community State Framework[/b].

Source code and release packages are published on GitHub when the project repository is linked from this Workshop item.

